# 20 Newsgroups protocol source audit: partial acquisition

The fixed source task completed successfully as an execution task and produced
its declared manifest.  Three scikit-learn primary documentation pages were
acquired and hashed.  The original maintainer page returned HTTP 502 after
10.98 seconds; this is a source-access failure, not evidence about the page's
contents or dataset license.

The acquired official documentation establishes the following protocol facts:

- 20 native classes and 18,846 total text samples are exposed by the loader.
- The documented train/test split is based on messages posted before versus
  after a specific date; it is therefore retained rather than randomized.
- Headers, signature-like footers, and quoted text can leak class-related
  metadata.  All three removal filters will be applied symmetrically, with the
  documented caveat that footers and quotes are heuristic and imperfect.
- The official sparse example fits TF-IDF on training text and only transforms
  test text, using `sublinear_tf=True`, `max_df=0.5`, `min_df=5`, and English
  stop words.  These settings are frozen for the first native pilot.
- The BSD markers on the acquired pages cover scikit-learn documentation and
  example code; they do not by themselves establish a license for the message
  corpus.  Dataset-use language remains unresolved until the maintainer page is
  recovered or its absence is explicitly recorded.

One bounded repair will try the HTTP and `www` variants of the same maintainer
page.  It will not request any dataset archive.  Failure of both variants ends
this source-recovery branch; it must not trigger repeated network retries.

